      "side": 0,
      "message": "If we remove this then we have another bug -- multi-byte memmap variables may be updated in between ACPI byte reads. How about this instead?\n\n- If the mutex is unlocked, we lock it from interrupt context.\n- Otherwise, we disable the acpi_0_interrupt, and kick off a new deferred task.\n- The deferred task locks the mutex and re-enables acpi_0_interrupt.\n- We can still unlock the mutex from interrupt context.\n\nWhat do you think?",
      "revId": "dd1987051b473e74dab3490921d0cc77c76fcac3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "deae1265_c6bd6225",
        "filename": "common/acpi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 184,
      "author": {
        "id": 1118349
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-06-18T18:03:49Z",
      "side": 0,
      "message": "I see the issue with multi-byte memmap variables. With current design EC-\u003eAP variables reading, options are limited.\nI prefer avoiding any mutex operations in interrupt context. And, I am for deferring all the processing to deferred task. How about this:\nOn ACPI_BURST_ENABLE:\n1. Defer acpi_burst_enable() with ZERO timeout.\n2. Defer acpi_burst_timeout() with 1s.\nOn ACPI_BURST_DISABLE:\n1. Defer acpi_burst_disable() with ZERO timeout.\n\nAnd, acpi_burst_timeout() just calls acpi_burst_disable().\nYou can have mutex lock in acpi_burst_enable(), mutex unlock in acpi_burst_disable().",
      "parentUuid": "5e6dc2eb_3d0d5b3f",
      "revId": "dd1987051b473e74dab3490921d0cc77c76fcac3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7e9c462a_c172460a",
        "filename": "common/acpi.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 184,
      "author": {
        "id": 1001696
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-06-18T18:22:21Z",
      "side": 0,
      "message": "That sounds fine, but we need to make sure that acpi_burst_enable is called before we get another ACPI READ command, so we probably should disable handling of the acpi_0_interrupt on ACPI_BURST_ENABLE, and then reenable it from acpi_burst_enable().\n\nAlso, it may be best to have acpi_burst_enable(),  acpi_burst_timeout() and  acpi_burst_disable() all be a single deferred function (maybe with a static state variable) because there is overhead involved for each separate deferred function we add.",
      "parentUuid": "deae1265_c6bd6225",
      "revId": "dd1987051b473e74dab3490921d0cc77c76fcac3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    }
